Certainly! Here's an example of how to sort an array of objects based on multiple properties:

```javascript
const movies = [
  { title: 'The Shawshank Redemption', year: 1994, director: 'Frank Darabont' },
  { title: 'The Godfather', year: 1972, director: 'Francis Ford Coppola' },
  { title: 'The Dark Knight', year: 2008, director: 'Christopher Nolan' },
  { title: '12 Angry Men', year: 1957, director: 'Sidney Lumet' },
];

movies.sort((a, b) => {
  if (a.director === b.director) {
    return b.year - a.year;
  } else {
    return a.director.localeCompare(b.director);
  }
});
```

In this example, we're sorting the `movies` array first by the `director` property in ascending alphabetical order, and then by the `year` property in descending order. 

We pass a compare function to the `sort` method that compares two objects `a` and `b`. The function first compares the `director` property of `a` and `b` using the `localeCompare` method. If they're the same, the function then compares the `year` property of `a` and `b` by subtracting `b.year` from `a.year` to get a descending order.

This results in the `movies` array sorted by `director` first and `year` second.
